y_m"},{"kind":"bar","title":"From enterprise value to equity value","x":["Present value of 5 years of cash flow","Present value of terminal value","= Enterprise value","− Debt","+ Cash","= Equity value"],"series":[{"name":"Value","values":[553556.0,1578480.0,2132040.0,-78114.0,36305.0,2090230.0]}],"fmt":"money_m"}],"findings":["With your assumptions the DCF gives a model value of 143,83 $ per share; the latest price is 356,27 $ (−59,6 %).","WACC 9,5 %, terminal growth 3,0 %, year-1 growth 5,5 %, operating margin 32,2 %.","Moving WACC by ±1 pt and terminal growth by ±0.5 pt gives 118,18 $ – 184,71 $; the range matters more than one number.","74,0 % of the value comes after year 5; long-run assumptions dominate.","The terminal value equals about 11,9× year-5 EBITDA; sanity-check it against peer EV/EBITDA in the Comps analysis.","Industry check (Damodaran, Computers/Peripherals): WACC 9,5 % vs industry 9,7 %, operating margin 32,2 % vs industry 22,5 %.","The model value is not a price target: a gap to the market price means the market assumes different growth, margins or risk than you. Use the Reverse DCF to see which."],"notes":["“Type” on every row: Reported = 10-K or price; Calculated = arithmetic on reported figures; Assumption = your choice; Model output = what the model returns with those assumptions.","FCFF = operating income × (1 − tax) + D&A − capex − increase in working capital. Cash flows are discounted at year end; terminal value uses the Gordon growth model: FCFF₅ × (1 + g) / (WACC − g).","Debt, cash and minority interests from the latest 10-K; shares from the cover page (else diluted average). Operating leases are not treated as debt because the lease cost is in operating income; unexercised options are not deducted, so the value can be somewhat high.","This is a model of your assumptions, not investment advice or a price target."],"sources":[{"label":"Apple 10-K FY2026 (filed 2026-11-03)","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/320193/000032019326